    level: "beginner",
    content: "## 🔥 Myth vs Reality: Client-Server Architecture Explained\n\n### ❌ The Myth\n\"It's just too complex to implement correctly in a fast-moving team.\"\n\n### ✅ The Reality\nCAP theorem forces a choice between consistency and availability during partitions.\n\nWhen you actually look at the patterns, it's elegant and straightforward.\n### Proof:\n\n```js\n// Rate limiter using Token Bucket (conceptual)\nclass TokenBucket {\n  constructor(capacity, refillRatePerSec) {\n    this.capacity = capacity;\n    this.tokens = capacity;\n    this.refillRate = refillRatePerSec;\n    setInterval(() => { this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + this.refillRate); }, 1000);\n  }\n  consume() { if (this.tokens > 0) { this.tokens--; return true; } return false; }\n}\n```\n\n### Best Practices\n- Design for failure — every component will eventually fail; plan for it.\n- Prefer simple proven components over clever custom solutions.\n\n\n---\n\n\n👉 **The Bottom Line:** Great system design is about asking the right questions and making explicit trade-offs."
